WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.490
SPEAKER: At just about
every organization,

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:05.910
it's someone's job to prepare
slide decks that contain lots

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.080
of informative data points.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.220
Now this process often involves
writing queries and then

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.160
manually adding the
results into each slide.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.210
But who wants to spend all
that time copying and pasting?

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:21.900
Well, we're about to make
your life a whole lot easier.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.060
In this video, we'll
walk through how

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.700
you can use Google Apps
Scripts to automate

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.440
data driven slide development.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.380
Let's imagine you're a data
analyst on the e-commerce team

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.630
for a huge retailer.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.570
Each week, you're tasked
with preparing Google Slides

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.840
for the director to present
to the broader team.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.800
The problem, this is a
super time consuming task.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.190
You're going to need to query
the e-commerce data to come up

00:00:56.190 --> 00:01:00.000
with the total sales, new
customers, number of orders,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.130
and repeat purchase rate.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:02.850
But wait.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:04.180
That's not it.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:06.690
There are more than 20
different departments,

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.270
and each one needs
their own slide.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:12.030
My thumb and index
fingers are hurting just

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:16.560
thinking about all those
Control-C and Control-Vs.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.720
Lucky for you, your
company already

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.590
has their e-commerce data
living inside of BigQuery.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:25.200
And one of the many great
things about BigQuery

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.510
is the flexibility
it offers in terms

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:30.330
of interacting with your data.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:34.440
BigQuery has a variety of
APIs, client libraries, and out

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.500
of the box integrations with
different Google services,

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.700
all of which make it easy
to access your data where

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.920
and when you need it.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.070
In this tutorial, we'll
be using BigQuery's API

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:50.430
as a service that's natively
available in Google Apps

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:51.580
Script.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:52.080
All right.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:52.830
Ready?

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:55.300
Let's jump in.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.740
So the first step in automating
the development of these slides

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.130
is to define our
metrics using SQL.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:05.430
Let's say it will calculate
total sales for each slide

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:10.110
by filtering our orders to a
specific department on a given

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:15.900
week, then summing up the total
sales of all the items ordered.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.790
Now, to ensure
we're consistently

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:20.550
calculating our
metrics, we can go ahead

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:25.110
and create a user-defined
function inside of BigQuery.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.960
With our function defined,
we can run it any time

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.690
we need to calculate
total sales simply

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:34.860
by feeding in a department
name and week timestamp.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.170
But what about actually
pushing the results

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.080
of this function into a slide?

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.810
One option for programmatically
developing slides

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:47.460
is to create a template slide
that we'll use for formatting.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:50.400
Here, I've created a
pretty simple template

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.580
where variables that will
be programmatically pushed

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:57.630
into our slide are surrounded
by distinguishable characters,

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:00.900
like these double
curly brackets.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.110
In my template, I can control
things like where on the slide

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:05.940
these variables
should be printed

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.040
and the color of the text.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:09.540
Great.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.300
So we have our template
all ready to go.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.400
Now to actually run
the calculations

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.410
and populate this
template, we'll

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.900
turn to Google Apps Script.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.270
Google Apps Script is
a scripting environment

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:24.210
for lightweight JavaScript
development within the Google

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.760
Workspace platform.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.100
Inside the Apps
Script IDE, we're

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.890
able to add Google
services to our program

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:34.330
directly through the UI.

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:36.720
This will make
interacting with the APIs

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.860
much easier since there's less
setup than using their HTTP

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.110
interfaces.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.780
Once we've added the
services we'll be using,

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:50.880
BigQuery, Drive, and Slides,
we're ready to start coding.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.390
First, we'll need to make a
copy of the template slide deck

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:58.170
to use as our
weekly presentation.

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:02.490
Next, we'll loop through each
department, create a new slide,

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:05.580
and then call this function.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.220
Zooming in a bit, you can
see that this function

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:13.170
goes into our new slide and
iterates through each metric.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.290
For each metric, we'll
use this helper function

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:19.860
to call the BigQuery
API and run the UDS

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:23.080
that we created earlier.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:25.290
Finally, we'll replace
the string containing

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:27.780
the curly brackets
and our metric name

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.990
with the result from BigQuery.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:32.910
When the loop
completes, we'll have

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:36.120
one slide for each department
with all our template

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:39.270
and metrics filled
in appropriately.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.820
And the finishing
touch, since you

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:44.370
need this available for the
director at the beginning

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:48.510
of each week, let's go ahead and
add a trigger to run the script

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:51.150
every Monday morning.

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:54.030
With integrations
across Google services,

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:57.270
you can deliver your data
wherever and whenever

00:04:57.270 --> 00:04:59.580
the business needs it.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.280
Leveraging Google Apps
Scripts to automate data

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:06.360
fueled document development
opens up huge opportunities

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:09.870
to save analysts time when it
comes to quarterly business

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:13.110
reviews or financial reporting.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:14.550
Ready to get started?

